<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monemvasia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Malvasia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Monemvasia is a fortified town on a rocky island off the south-eastern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, joined to the mainland by a causeway. Founded in the 6th century, it was a strong Byzantine port that fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II of Villehardouin</a> in 1248 and was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> in 1262. It later belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and under its Italian name, Malvasia, it gave its name to malmsey wine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monemvasia.jpg" alt="Monemvasia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Katsikas pantelis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%9A%CE%B1%CF%83%CF%84%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%80%CE%BF%CE%BB%CE%B9%CF%84%CE%B5%CE%B9%CE%B1_%CE%BC%CE%BF%CE%BD%CE%B5%CE%BC%CE%B2%CE%B1%CF%83%CE%B9%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
